window.SEARCH_DOCS = [{"id":0,"url":"index.html","title":"Authentic Memory Docs","section":"","text":"Authentic Memory docs This manual describes how our technology is designed and built. Authentic Memory is protecting historical records in a synthetic age. We provide non-profit infrastructure to ensure that humanity’s historical record remains distinguishable from AI-generated content. We open-source our software under Apache 2.0 license for others to inspect and re-use. If you are new to Authentic Memory , check out a demonstration . If you would like to verify an Authentic Memory proof record visit verify.authenticmemory.org . If you manage an archival collection and would like to use Authentic Memory anchoring , see this explanation and instructions . If you manage an archival collection and want to understand the tech behind Authentic Memory before committing to a collaboration, this technology overview page provides the clearest explanation. Otherwise, this manual provides the most detailed and current technical documentation ."},{"id":1,"url":"requirements/index.html","title":"Software requirements","section":"","text":"Software requirements"},{"id":2,"url":"requirements/standards/index.html","title":"Standards","section":"Software requirements","text":"Standards"},{"id":3,"url":"requirements/theory/index.html","title":"Theory and research","section":"Software requirements","text":"Theory and research"},{"id":4,"url":"requirements/user/index.html","title":"User requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"User requirements"},{"id":5,"url":"requirements/data/index.html","title":"Data requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"Data requirements"},{"id":6,"url":"requirements/functional/index.html","title":"Functional requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"Functional requirements"},{"id":7,"url":"requirements/quality/index.html","title":"Quality requirements","section":"Software requirements","text":"Quality requirements"},{"id":8,"url":"requirements/quality/security.html","title":"Security","section":"Software requirements › Quality requirements","text":"Security"},{"id":9,"url":"requirements/quality/performance.html","title":"Performance","section":"Software requirements › Quality requirements","text":"Performance"},{"id":10,"url":"requirements/quality/scalability.html","title":"Scalability","section":"Software requirements › Quality requirements","text":"Scalability"},{"id":11,"url":"decisions/index.html","title":"Architectural decisions","section":"","text":"Architectural decisions This section explains the architecture options and decisions made by the Authentic Memory team. They are each presented as an Architectural Decision Record (ADR)."},{"id":12,"url":"decisions/anchoring/index.html","title":"Timestamp anchoring","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"Timestamp anchoring"},{"id":13,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-010-bitcoin-blockchain-time-anchor.html","title":"Bitcoin blockchain time anchor","section":"Architectural decisions › Timestamp anchoring","text":"Bitcoin blockchain time anchor Architectural Decision Record (ADR) 010 Description Use the Bitcoin blockchain as the primary time anchor for Authentic Memory proof records. Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference The core guarantee of Authentic Memory’s Open Proof Record is existence : a publicly verifiable lower bound on the date by which a file existed in exactly its current form. The evidentiary value of that lower bound depends on when it is created, because a timestamp made before indistinguishable synthetic media became possible supports an inference that a later one cannot. The time anchor therefore has to: remain verifiable for decades, beyond the lifetime of Authentic Memory and of any single institution; be checkable by anyone, without an account, fee, permission or trust in Authentic Memory; resist retroactive alteration, including by well-resourced adversaries such as organised denial campaigns. Decision Anchor every Open Proof Record in the Bitcoin blockchain. The hash of each signed OPR (or of a Merkle root over a batch of them) is committed to a Bitcoin transaction through the OpenTimestamps protocol. The resulting proof is stored in the OPR’s timestamps array. Rationale Cost of rewriting history grows with time. Under Bitcoin’s proof-of-work model, the cost of rewriting confirmed transactions grows exponentially with their age. Track record. Bitcoin has operated continuously for over fifteen years without a successful attack on confirmed blocks. Permissionless and public. It is fully public and independently verifiable without any account, fee or permission. No maintaining authority. Long-term temporal proof rests on historical block headers, which are immutable, verifiable offline and bound to no authority that has to survive. A record stays verifiable even if Authentic Memory as an organisation does not. Retroactive protection for signatures. Because the anchor fixes the date, a future break of Ed25519 cannot produce a forged signature that appears to have been anchored in a 2026 block. Assumptions Bitcoin block headers will remain publicly available and the chain’s proof-of-work security will continue to hold for the evidentiary lifetime of the proofs. SHA-256, on which both Bitcoin and OpenTimestamps depend, remains collision- and pre-image-resistant. Partner institutions, funders and public authorities will accept a public blockchain as a time anchor. Alternatives Considered RFC 3161 timestamping authorities and certificate chains (the C2PA model). Rejected as the primary anchor: their validity depends on authorities and certificates staying valid, requiring periodic re-timestamping and preservation of validation data, an obligation that cannot lapse over fifty to a hundred years. RFC 3161 remains a candidate additional anchor . bloXberg (scientific blockchain). Considered as a supplementary ledger for redundancy, not as a replacement. Certificate Transparency logs, an Authentic Memory transparency log and AT Protocol feed Proposed in the to-be architecture as additional, independent anchors, not as substitutes. Zeitwerk (federated hourly co-signatures). Would suit a second anchor if it became established infrastructure but could probably be discontinued. Authentic Memory is hosting a mirror repository of Zeitwerk timestamps. Consequences / Implications Easier: proofs can be validated with only the OPR, the file, the institution’s public key and public Bitcoin headers; Authentic Memory is not in the validity path. Latency: anchoring is not instantaneous. Proofs are “pending” until a Bitcoin block confirms them and must then be upgraded. Lower bound only: the anchor proves existence no later than the block date. It says nothing about integrity before anchoring (Tier 2 evidence) or about content truthfulness."},{"id":14,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-020-opentimestamps-time-anchor.html","title":"Opentimestamps time anchor","section":"Architectural decisions › Timestamp anchoring","text":"Opentimestamps time anchor Architectural Decision Record (ADR) 020 Title Anchor OPRs to Bitcoin through OpenTimestamps calendars with Merkle aggregation Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference Authentic Memory has selected the Bitcoin blockchain network as its primary time anchor. Writing one Bitcoin transaction per record is impractical: institutions may anchor tens of millions of files per year, and each transaction carries a fee and confirmation delay. A mechanism is needed that commits very large numbers of digests to Bitcoin cheaply, produces a portable proof per record, and can be verified with open tooling. Decision The institutional portal aggregates the digests of signed OPRs in a signing batch into a Merkle tree and submits them to the public OpenTimestamps (OTS) calendars . The calendars aggregate submissions from all users into a further Merkle tree and commit its root in a Bitcoin transaction (C8). Submission goes to multiple calendar servers with automatic retry . The portal later upgrades pending proofs to complete Bitcoin proofs once the commitment is confirmed. The OTS proof is stored in the OPR’s timestamps array with its method, status, submission date and, once confirmed, block height. Rationale OpenTimestamps is an open protocol with independent implementations, usable without an account or fee. Aggregation lets a single Bitcoin commitment cover arbitrarily many records, so anchoring cost does not grow with collection size. OTS uses SHA-256, the same hash used for Authentic Memory file fingerprints. A completed OTS proof is verified against Bitcoin block headers alone. The calendars are needed to create and upgrade proofs, not to verify them. Assumptions The public, volunteer-run OTS calendars continue to accept submissions and publish their commitments to Bitcoin. At least one calendar is reachable at submission time. The automatic retry absorbs temporary outages. The portal’s upgrade job runs until every pending proof is confirmed. Alternatives Considered Authentic Memory writing its own Bitcoin transactions, or running its own calendar. Nope. RFC 3161 selected as second option. Consequences / Implications Easier: anchoring scales to large batches at negligible marginal cost. Harder: the OTS calendars are a dependency for creating proofs, not for verifying them. Until a pending proof is upgraded (usually within hours), it relies on at least one of the calendars that received it. If all fail, the batch is resubmitted with a later date. Overdue pending proofs need alerting. Running Authentic Memory’s own calendar, estimated at €10k a year including the operator, would remove the creation dependency. The Bitcoin chain stores only a Merkle root, so a lost OPR cannot be rebuilt from the blockchain. Institution-held copies and replication are essential. The service depends on volunteer infrastructure it does not control."},{"id":15,"url":"decisions/anchoring/ADR-030-rfc-3161-time-anchor.html","title":"RFC 3161 time anchor","section":"Architectural decisions › Timestamp anchoring","text":"RFC 3161 time anchor Architectural Decision Record (ADR) 030 Description Use eIDAS-qualified RFC 3161 time-stamps from two unrelated providers as the second, Bitcoin-independent time anchor for Authentic Memory proof records. Status Proposed Context / Requirement Reference The Bitcoin blockchain, reached through OpenTimestamps calendars is the only time anchor for Authentic Memory. Every live proof therefore shares one failure mode. Authentic Memory has a funder commitment to deliver a second independent timestamping system where “every batch goes to both, the verifier shows both.” The second anchor therefore has to: fail for different reasons than Bitcoin and the OpenTimestamps calendars; carry recognised legal effect, which the Bitcoin anchor does not, for institutions with legal retention duties; stay verifiable by third parties, offline, without Authentic Memory; receive only batch roots, never file digests stay cheap and light to operate for a small non-profit that anchors a few batch roots a day, without dependence on any one vendor. Decision Anchor every batch a second time with RFC 3161 time-stamp tokens through an eIDAS-qualified time-stamping authorities. Mechanism. The token is issued over the same value that is submitted to OpenTimestamps for the batch. That value is sent as the messageImprint with the SHA-256 identifier and is not hashed again. Entitlement. Keep a qualified source under contract with prepaid terms, so that the service is a right and not a tolerated use of an open endpoint. Evidence stored. Each token is stored in the Open Proof Record with the signer’s certificate chain, the revocation evidence current at the time, the policy identifier, the attested time and a digest of the national trusted list used to confirm qualified status. Record format. The OPR carries a list of anchors typed by mechanism, in place of the single OpenTimestamps object. Each entry states its trust basis and what it relies on. Verifier. The verifier shows each anchor on its own line with its type, attested time, precision and trust basis. It never merges anchors into one result, and it reports an unknown anchor type as “not evaluated”, not as a failure. Renewal. Each time-stamp is renewed before its signer certificate expires or its algorithm is deprecated, following the Evidence Record method (RFC 4998). Rationale Independent failure. A signed token from a supervised authority involves no blockchain. It fails for different reasons than Bitcoin and the OpenTimestamps calendars. Legal standing. A qualified electronic time-stamp carries a presumption of the accuracy of its date and of the integrity of the data in every EU Member State (eIDAS Article 41). Mutual protection. The Bitcoin anchor later shows that a token existed before its certificate expired. The token gives a time to the second and a legal effect that Bitcoin cannot. Standard and simple. RFC 3161 is a published IETF standard. No custom protocol or verifier logic is needed beyond certificate-path checking, stock openssl and curl can be used. Privacy. The protocol time-stamps a hash only and forbids the authority from recording the requester. Only the batch root leaves the service. Cost and schedule. Between nothing and about €65 a year at current volume, and it can be in production before 31 December 2026. Assumptions The chosen authority remains on its national trusted list with “granted” status for qualified time-stamps. The operators accept this use. For example, Sectigo’s disclosure statement permits use for any application needing proof that a datum existed. DigiCert Europe’s policy reserves the right to limit use to its certificate holders, so written confirmation or a contract is still to be obtained. Both authorities publish revocation data that Authentic Memory may archive. SHA-256 (see ADR-030 ) and the authorities’ signature algorithms stay sound until the first renewal. The OPR’s anchor fields sit outside the institution’s signed form, so anchors can be added and renewed without a new institutional signature. eIDAS qualification is persuasive, though not binding, for institutions outside the EU. Alternatives Considered Non-qualified RFC 3161 time-stamps DigiStamp FreeTSA, certificate authorities’ public endpoints. Certificate Transparency anchor Rejected as the mandatory anchor: logs accept certificates, not hashes; a signed certificate timestamp is a promise to include, not a proof; the evidence bundle is large and decays as logs retire; it depends on a certificate authority’s tolerance, has no legal standing and needs custom verifier code. Services that rely on Bitcoin A second OpenTimestamps proof or calendar, Let’s Seal, Project Timestamper, the Bitcoin leg of OriginStamp. Rejected: the same anchor under another name. Other blockchains The Ethereum leg of OriginStamp, or writing directly to Ethereum, Algorand, Tezos, Cardano or Arweave. Rejected: not Bitcoin in name, but the same class of risk as the first anchor, with key and fee management and no legal standing. Zeitwerk A good design, but a prototype with no co-signers, no written specification and no continuation funding. To be reassessed only against the published acceptance criteria. OriginTrail and WordProof Rejected as not independently verifiable: OriginTrail needs tokens and a node, its on-chain commitment is undocumented and it cannot be verified offline; WordProof is a publisher service on EOS with vendor-dependent verification. C2PA and IdentityTrusts Not time sources. C2PA stores RFC 3161 tokens but does not issue time; IdentityTrusts produces signed consent records with no time attestation. Guardtime KSI. Needs neither a blockchain nor a certificate to verify, which would make it the most independent option. Deferred for cost, a single-vendor format and unconfirmed trusted-list status. Candidate for a later third kind of anchor . Sigstore Rekor. Built for software signing; version 2 has no time of its own and retires log shards about every six months. A self-hosted time-stamping authority or transparency log. Gives no independent trust. IETF SCITT, Roughtime and randomness beacons (drand, NIST). No neutral public service, experimental status, or proof of “not before” only. eIDAS qualified electronic ledgers. Defined in law since 2024, but no provider was found in operation. Publishing roots widely (DOI deposit, Internet Archive, AT Proto feed, print). Nearly free and suitable as an extra, but its trust is social, so it is not the formal second anchor. Consequences / Implications Easier: every proof record gains time evidence with legal effect and second-level precision, and the verifier can show two anchors with different trust bases, which answers the single-anchor critique. Institutions under legal retention rules can relate a record to practice they already know (qualified time-stamps, TR-ESOR, Evidence Records). Extensible: adding further anchor types later needs a new entry type, not a new record format. Renewal becomes a standing duty: certificates expire and algorithms age. Evidence must be stored in full at anchoring time and re-stamped on a schedule. If renewal lapses, the token’s value rests on the Bitcoin anchor. A trust list must be governed: the verifier needs a published, versioned list of accepted authorities and a rule for judging a token against the trusted list as it stood at the time. This is a new governance duty. Record format and verifier change: the proof record schema, the batch-close workflow, the web verifier and the CLI verifier must all handle a list of anchors, and the CLI verifier must check RFC 3161 tokens offline. The forms used for signing and for time-stamping must exclude the anchor list. Records grow: a token with its chain adds about 4 to 6 kB per provider. Whether the chain is stored per record or once per batch is undecided. Shared technology: RFC 3161 tokens rest on X.509 certificates. They are independent of Bitcoin but not of each other in kind. Existing records: records already issued can be time-stamped now, but the token proves existence only from the day it is made. A trusted third party enters the design: the first principle of ADR-010 (timestamping on Bitcoin) is that no authority has to survive. This decision accepts an authority because it is additional to, and checkable against, the anchor that needs none."},{"id":16,"url":"decisions/cryptography/index.html","title":"Cryptography","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"Cryptography"},{"id":17,"url":"decisions/cryptography/ADR-100-sha256-file-fingerprint.html","title":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory","section":"Architectural decisions › Cryptography","text":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory ADR-003-sha256-file-fingerprint.md Title Use SHA-256, computed locally or reused from existing fixity records, as the file fingerprint Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference A GAMI proof must identify one exact file without the file ever leaving institutional custody ( what we deliberately do not do ). Collections reach the petabyte range, so the fingerprint must be fast to compute and, where possible, reuse work institutions have already done. Related requirements: U-02 (calculate digests locally without changing source files), U-03 (reuse trustworthy existing digests), U-04 (resumable preparation) in system requirements . Decision Fingerprint every file with SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) . The hash is computed inside the institution’s infrastructure, by GAMI Hash ( ADR-011 ) or by the institution’s own systems. Where an OAIS-conformant preservation system already holds SHA-256 fixity values (for example Archivematica, Preservica), those values may be reused instead of rehashing. SHA-256 is also used to hash GPRs for anchoring and in public verification ( ADR-015 ). Rationale From system workflow , system security and system scalability : Suitable properties: determinism, avalanche effect, pre-image resistance and collision resistance. Ecosystem alignment: the same function is used by OpenTimestamps, C2PA, Archivematica and Preservica, so existing fixity hashes can be reused and the GPR digest feeds OTS directly. Long-term security: 128-bit effective security even against quantum search (Grover); no known practical weaknesses. Throughput: about 500 MB/s per CPU core; roughly 1 PB hashed in about three days on one 8-core server. Assumptions SHA-256 remains secure for the evidentiary lifetime of the proofs, or a weakness will be announced early enough for the renewal chain to re-anchor with a stronger algorithm ( post-quantum resilience ). Reused fixity values are current and correctly bound to the file being anchored (challenged by risk T-13). Alternatives Considered No alternative primary hash function is recorded as having been evaluated. SHA-3 is named as the post-quantum re-hashing algorithm for the planned renewal chain, not as a replacement for the file fingerprint ( system security ). Perceptual fingerprinting is described as a future extension for matching re-encoded or cropped derivatives, complementing exact-byte hashing ( matched-fragment attribution ). Consequences / Implications Easier: institutions with SHA-256 fixity skip the hashing phase entirely. Verification needs only the file and standard tooling. Exact-byte matching only: re-encoded, cropped or screenshot copies never match, and readers may read “not found” as “fake” (risk T-07). Fixity reuse is weaker than it looks: only Archivematica stores SHA-256 by default among surveyed systems, and no current pilot uses it (risk T-29). Reused values may be stale or bound to the wrong file (risk T-13), so reconciliation with sample re-hashing before signing is required (U-34). Disclosure: publishing file hashes lets anyone confirm whether a known file is held (risk T-27). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-009 : read-only integration that relies on reusing fixity. ADR-011 : GAMI Hash. ADR-015 : hashing in the reader’s browser. Risks T-07, T-13, T-27, T-29 in the risk register ."},{"id":18,"url":"decisions/cryptography/ADR-110-ed25519-institutional-signatures.html","title":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory","section":"Architectural decisions › Cryptography","text":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory ADR-004-ed25519-institutional-signatures.md Title Use Ed25519 for institutional signatures on GPRs Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference A hash identifies a file but does not say who vouches for it. The institutional attribution guarantee requires that a named institution signs each proof with a key under its exclusive control, verifiable by anyone against the institution’s published public key ( core guarantees ). The scheme must be widely implemented so that independent verifiers can be built in any language. Related requirements: U-07 (signing officer signs the authorized batch), U-13 (independent verification) in system requirements . Decision Sign GPRs with Ed25519 (RFC 8032) , producing 64-byte signatures. The public key is published through the institution’s DID document ( ADR-008 ). The private key is held on a hardware security key ( ADR-005 ). Rationale From system workflow and system security : Ed25519 is an established elliptic-curve scheme widely deployed in SSH, TLS and the Signal protocol. It provides 128-bit classical security with compact 64-byte signatures. It has mature implementations in all major programming languages, supporting the goal that verification can be fully reimplemented by third parties. It is supported by the hardware security keys used for institutional signing. Assumptions The private key remains under the institution’s sole control; otherwise a valid signature no longer implies institutional authorship. Quantum computers capable of running Shor’s algorithm against Ed25519 will not exist before the renewal chain is in place. Even then, the anchor date bounds any forgery: a forger cannot create a signature that was anchored in an earlier Bitcoin block ( post-quantum resilience ). Alternatives Considered No alternative classical signature scheme (for example ECDSA or RSA) is recorded as having been evaluated. SLH-DSA (FIPS 205) is planned for signing the aggregated tree in the renewal chain, not for per-record signing. It is not yet deployed ( standards compliance ). Consequences / Implications Easier: small signatures and fast verification (under 1 ms per signature, per the performance table ). Quantum vulnerability: Ed25519, like the CT and TSA signatures considered for additional anchors, is quantum-vulnerable. Migration may require re-signing, and what the renewal chain preserves has not been modelled (risk T-12). Algorithm identifiers should be kept in every record for agility. Signed bytes: in the as-is implementation Ed25519 signs WebAuthn authenticator data plus a client-data hash, not the canonical GPR directly ( ADR-005 ). Verifiers must handle both forms until the proof contract is frozen (D-02, risk T-01). Encoding: a production record stores its signature in hex where schema v1 requires Base64 (U-25, risk T-01). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-005 : hardware-key signing through WebAuthn. ADR-007 : canonicalization of the signed content. ADR-008 : publication of the public key. Open decision D-02 (proof contract); risks T-01, T-12 in the risk register ."},{"id":19,"url":"decisions/cryptography/ADR-120-hardware-key-webauthn-signing.html","title":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory","section":"Architectural decisions › Cryptography","text":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory ADR-005-hardware-key-webauthn-signing.md Title Sign through WebAuthn on institution-held hardware security keys, with Merkle-root batch signing Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference The institutional signature only means something if the private key is under the institution’s sole control. GAMI must never hold institutional private keys ( key management ). At the same time, institutions anchor large batches, and asking a signing officer to approve thousands of records one by one is not workable. Related requirements: U-07 (signing officer inspects and signs the authorized batch), U-08 (manage authorized signers and continuity), U-40 (key custody usable without sharing keys or writing down PINs) in system requirements . Decision Custody: the institution holds its Ed25519 signing key on a hardware security key (YubiKey in pilots; an HSM is possible), with a primary and a backup key. GAMI never holds private keys ( identity and key management ). Mechanism: signatures are produced through WebAuthn in the institutional portal. The WebAuthn challenge carries the record hash or the batch hash. The signed bytes are the authenticator data plus a hash of the client data ( cryptography and proof format ). Batching: batch signing builds a Merkle tree (RFC 6962 style) over the records in a signing batch and signs its root, so one hardware-key touch covers a whole batch ( GAMI app ). Rationale The private key never leaves the hardware, so neither GAMI nor a compromised server can sign on the institution’s behalf. WebAuthn is a browser standard, so the hosted portal can request signatures from a hardware key without installing software at the institution. Merkle-root signing makes the signing effort independent of batch size while letting each record carry a proof of inclusion. Inferred, not stated in the source documentation: requiring user presence (a physical touch) for every signature gives a clear, auditable act of institutional approval. Assumptions Signing officers can set up and operate hardware keys correctly. Pilot evidence challenges this: setup took 45 minutes instead of 5, and keys were shared and PINs written down (risk T-15). Supported browsers behave consistently for WebAuthn (challenged by risk T-35). Verifiers can reconstruct and check the WebAuthn signed bytes. GAMI Verify checks “Ed25519/WebAuthn signatures” ( GAMI Verify ). Alternatives Considered Signing the canonical GPR directly with Ed25519. This is the method in the architecture description ( signing process ). The as-is implementation signs WebAuthn data instead; how the two relate is open (D-02). Per-record signing. Used in one of the four source descriptions of the GPR. Batch-root signing is used in the others (risk T-01). Hosted or managed keys, zero-knowledge hosted keys, an HSM profile for national archives, and tiered custody by institution capacity. Under discussion as options, not adopted (D-14, risk T-15). GAMI hashing and signing on the institution’s behalf (the pilot “media handover” pattern). Being ended because it conflicts with the trust model (risk O-15). Consequences / Implications Easier: strong key isolation, and one touch signs a whole batch. Harder: the signed bytes are WebAuthn structures, not the bare canonical GPR, which complicates the proof contract and independent verification (D-02, risk T-01). Bulk signing currently accepts only the primary key, so the backup key cannot be used for batches (D-14, risk T-15). A YubiKey rollout in production is a funder commitment for 31 Dec 2026. Key loss, compromise and insider misuse need playbooks and monitoring (risks T-16, T-17). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-004 : Ed25519. ADR-008 : publication of the public key. ADR-013 : hardware key required for signing. Open decisions D-02, D-14; risks T-01, T-15, T-16, T-17, T-35, O-15 in the risk register ."},{"id":20,"url":"decisions/cryptography/ADR-130-did-web-institutional-identity.html","title":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory","section":"Architectural decisions › Cryptography","text":"Architecture Decision Record - Authentic Memory ADR-008-did-web-institutional-identity.md Title Publish institutional signing keys as DIDs on the institution’s own domain (did:web, moving to did:webvh) Status Accepted Context / Requirement Reference Verifiers need to find the public key that signed a GPR, know that it belongs to the named institution, and know which key was valid on the signing date, including after rotations, revocations and domain changes. Memory institutions, especially national archives and memorial sites, are unlikely to accept that the authenticity of their records depends on membership of a centrally governed trust list ( relationship to C2PA ). Related requirements: U-17 (rotate, recover and retire authority with preserved history), U-20 (know what supports an institution’s identity), U-50 (resolver enforces did:webvh checks) in system requirements . Decision Each institution publishes its keys as a Decentralized Identifier on its own primary domain : did:web ( /.well-known/did.json ) for the first pilot institutions. did:webvh (DIF “did:web + Verifiable History”, version 1.0) as the target, published at /.well-known/did.jsonl alongside a plain did.json for compatibility. It provides a self-certifying identifier (SCID), a hash-chained log of every DID document version, pre-rotation and portable: true for domain moves ( key management ). The GPR’s key_id is a DID URL (for example did:web:kz-gedenkstaette-dachau.de#key-2026 ). Key revocation is published as a revocation date in the DID document, followed by rotation; annual rotation is expected. Rationale Sovereignty: each institution is its own trust root. Verification checks the institution’s own signature, not a central authorisation ( relationship to C2PA ). Verifiable key history: the key that signed a GPR is validated against the log entry authoritative at signing time, so records signed under retired keys stay verifiable. Resistance to domain takeover: whoever later controls the domain cannot forge a substitute identity or rewrite the log without breaking the chain. Pre-rotation: a compromised signing key does not permit an unauthorised rotation. Backwards compatibility: existing did:web tooling keeps working, and did:web identities can be upgraded in place. Assumptions Institutions control and maintain their primary domain and can publish files under /.well-known/ . The did:webvh specification and resolver libraries are mature enough for production. One resolver was found skipping timestamp checks (fixed upstream; risk T-18). A timestamped archive of partner DID logs can serve as a fallback when a domain is unavailable. Because the log is self-certifying, the archive only needs to be available, not trusted. Alternatives Considered X.509 certificates checked against a central trust list (the C2PA model). Rejected because it makes an institution’s authenticity depend on membership in a list governed by others ( relationship to C2PA ). Plain did:web only. Retained for the first pilots but weaker: it has no verifiable history, and its fallback after a domain loss requires trust in the archive (risk T-19). Consequences / Implications Easier: no certificate authority or live GAMI service is needed to verify an existing proof. Domain dependence: live key resolution fails when a domain lapses, is taken over or goes offline. The DID log archive fallback is documented but its shipped status is unconfirmed (risks T-03, T-19). Lookalike domains: a valid signature from a lookalike domain is cryptographically sound but misleading. A confirmed-domain directory, kept out of the verification path, is proposed (D-12, risk T-20). Result semantics: the verifier’s top-level result does not yet depend on key-authorization status (D-03, risk T-02, rated Critical). did:webvh protections such as witnesses and pre-rotation are optional by default and must be enabled per institution (risk T-18). Related Decisions / Notes ADR-004 , ADR-005 : the keys being published. ADR-016 : DID authorization checks in GAMI Verify. Open decisions D-03, D-12; risks T-02, T-16, T-18, T-19, T-20 in the risk register ."},{"id":21,"url":"decisions/proof-record/index.html","title":"Proof record","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"Proof record"},{"id":22,"url":"decisions/tech-stack/index.html","title":"Tech stack","section":"Architectural decisions","text":"Tech stack"},{"id":23,"url":"architecture/index.html","title":"Architectural design","section":"","text":"Architectural design Integer in mauris eu nibh euismod gravida. Duis ac tellus et risus vulputate vehicula. Donec lobortis risus a elit. Etiam tempor, ut ullamcorper ligula eu tempor congue, eros est euismod turpis."},{"id":24,"url":"architecture/view1.html","title":"View 1","section":"Architectural design","text":"View 1"},{"id":25,"url":"architecture/view2.html","title":"View2","section":"Architectural design","text":""},{"id":26,"url":"software/index.html","title":"Software components","section":"","text":"Software components"},{"id":27,"url":"software/gami-hash/index.html","title":"gami-hash","section":"Software components","text":"gami-hash"},{"id":28,"url":"software/gami-app/index.html","title":"gami-app","section":"Software components","text":"gami-app"},{"id":29,"url":"software/gami-verify/index.html","title":"gami-verify","section":"Software components","text":"gami-verify"},{"id":30,"url":"references/glossary/index.html","title":"Glossary","section":"References","text":"Glossary"},{"id":31,"url":"references/citations/index.html","title":"Citations","section":"References","text":"Citations"}];
